A reentrant function is one that can be safely called multiple times while it is running.
The following garden-variety swap function, for instance, is nonreentrant because it
has a temporary variable t whose value must not change before it is used to set the
final y variable:

void swap(int* x, int* y) {
  t = *x;
  *x = *y;
  // hardware interrupt might invoke isr() here while
  // the function has been called from main or other
  // non-interrupt code
  *y =  t;
}

void isr {
  int x=1, y=2;
  swap(&x, &y);
}

